const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/washer-repair/ge-not-spinning/";

const title = "GE Washer Not Spinning? Causes & Fix";
const description = "GE washer won't spin? On a top-loader the lid switch is the classic culprit; also a drain it must clear first, or the drive. Same-day Los Angeles, $89.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why won't my GE washer spin?",
    a: "On a GE top-loader the most common cause is the lid switch — the washer won't spin unless it senses the lid is closed, and that switch wears out. Other causes across GE washers are a drain problem it has to clear before spinning, a front-loader door that won't lock, an unbalanced load, and mechanical parts like the drive belt, motor, or (on older top-loaders) the motor coupling. Unlike Samsung or LG, most GE washers don't throw a specific no-spin code — GE is largely diagnosed by symptom, so a GE that won't spin usually just stops with no code, and you find the fault by testing the parts. Checking the lid switch, load balance, and drain is homeowner-level; drive and motor work is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "Does a GE washer show a code when it won't spin?",
    a: "Usually not. Most GE washers are diagnosed by symptom rather than a digital no-spin code — a GE that won't spin typically just stops with nothing on the display, and you find the fault by testing the lid switch, drain, door, and drive. Some newer GE models (UltraFast combos and certain Profile units) do show codes, but the classic GE top-loader doesn't, so 'there's no code' is normal for GE, not a sign the control is dead. That's why the diagnosis is hands-on: check the lid switch and the mechanical parts rather than waiting for a code that isn't coming."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the lid switch and why does it stop the spin?",
    a: "The lid switch is a safety on GE top-loaders that tells the control the lid is closed. The washer won't spin the drum with the lid open — for obvious safety reasons — so if the switch fails or its actuator is worn or misaligned, the control thinks the lid is up and refuses to spin, even with the lid firmly shut. It's one of the most common GE no-spin parts and an inexpensive one. You can often hear it: a good lid switch clicks when you press the lid down. If pressing the lid firmly and re-running the spin does nothing and there's no click, the switch is the prime suspect — a technician tests and replaces it."
  },
  {
    q: "Why won't my GE spin if it won't drain?",
    a: "A washer drains before it spins — it won't spin on a full tub because it can't balance and would sling water everywhere. So if your GE isn't draining, it never reaches the spin step, and 'won't drain' shows up as 'won't spin.' Check the drain first: the drain hose for a kink, the standpipe, and (on front-loaders) the pump filter. Clear the drain and the spin usually returns. If the tub is empty and it still won't spin, then it's the lid switch, door lock, or a drive part rather than a drain problem."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a GE washer that won't spin myself?",
    a: "Some of it. Redistribute an unbalanced load, make sure the machine is level, clear the drain (hose and, on front-loaders, the pump filter), and test the lid switch by pressing the lid firmly and listening for the click while you start a spin. Those cover the most common GE no-spin causes. What to leave to a tech: replacing the lid switch itself, the front-loader door lock, the drive belt or motor, or (on older top-loaders) the motor coupling — those need the cabinet opened and a meter. Always unplug the washer before reaching into the drain or switch area."
  }
];

