<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-spin GE top-loader is usually the lid switch — and there's rarely a code.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a GE washer won't spin, the top suspect on a top-loader is the lid switch: the washer won't spin unless it senses the lid is closed, and that switch wears out. Other causes are a drain problem it must clear before spinning, a front-loader door that won't lock, an unbalanced load, and drive parts — the belt, the motor, or an older top-loader's motor coupling. The GE difference is that most GE washers don't show a no-spin code; GE is diagnosed by symptom, so a GE that won't spin usually just stops with nothing on the display, and you find the fault by testing the parts. That's normal for GE, not a dead control. Testing the lid switch, balancing the load, and clearing the drain are homeowner-level; drive and motor work is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>No code? Normal for GE.</strong> Most GE washers are symptom-diagnosed — you check the lid switch, drain, door, and drive rather than read a code. A blank display isn't a broken control.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 LID SWITCH -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The lid switch — GE's classic no-spin part</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">On a GE top-loader, start here.</h2>

    <p>The lid switch is a safety that tells the control the lid is closed. A GE top-loader won't spin the drum with the lid open — for obvious reasons — so when the switch fails, or its actuator is worn or bent out of alignment, the control believes the lid is up and refuses to spin even with the lid firmly shut. It's one of the most common GE no-spin faults, and it's an inexpensive part.</p>

    <p>You can often check it yourself: a working lid switch <strong>clicks</strong> when you press the lid down at the switch location. Press the lid firmly closed, start a spin, and listen. If you hear the click and it still won't spin, look elsewhere (drain, drive). If there's no click and no spin, the lid switch is the prime suspect — a technician confirms it with a meter and replaces it. GE doesn't usually flag this with a code, which is exactly why it's a hands-on check.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a GE won't spin</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Lid switch (top-loader, check first)</h3>
      <p>The washer won't spin unless it senses the lid closed. A worn switch or actuator reads the lid as open. Listen for the click; no click, no spin points here. Inexpensive, common — tech confirms and replaces.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drain fault</h3>
      <p>It won't spin on a full tub, so a drain problem reads as a no-spin. Check the drain hose and (front-loaders) the pump filter. Clear the water and the spin usually returns.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door lock (front-loader)</h3>
      <p>A GE front-loader won't spin unless the door locks. If the door shuts but won't lock, the lock assembly has failed. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Unbalanced load</h3>
      <p>A single heavy item or an off-level machine can stop the spin from ramping up. Redistribute and level before assuming a part.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Drive belt, motor, or motor coupling</h3>
      <p>A worn belt or motor, or (on older GE top-loaders) a failed motor coupling, stops the spin mechanically with no code. A tech diagnosis with a meter.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before it's a repair.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Test the lid switch</strong> — press the lid firmly closed, start a spin, and listen for the click. No click, no spin points at the switch.</li>
      <li><strong>Redistribute the load</strong> evenly and make sure the machine is level.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the drain</strong> — unplug first, check the drain hose for a kink and the standpipe; on front-loaders check the pump filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Check a front-loader door</strong> shuts fully with nothing caught in the seal.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> the lid-switch listen-test, load balance, drain, and door check are safe. Replacing the lid switch, door lock, drive belt, motor, or motor coupling means opening the cabinet with a meter — our techs' work. Unplug first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SIBLING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Same symptom, different code system</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">No-spin is universal — GE just doesn't announce it.</h2>

    <p>Every washer drains and locks before it spins, so unbalance, drain, and door/lid faults cause no-spin on every brand. What's different is that a Samsung will hand you a code (UR, 5C, dC) while a GE mostly makes you find it by symptom. If yours is actually a Samsung, our <a href="/services/washer-repair/samsung-not-spinning/">Samsung washer not spinning</a> page walks the code system. And if the GE's real issue is the drain, the general <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> guide covers the full drain path.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Lid switch clicks, drain clear — still won't spin?</h2>

    <p>When the lid switch clicks, the load's balanced, the drain is clear, and the GE still won't spin, the fault is mechanical — the door lock, the drive belt, the motor, or a motor coupling. Because GE is symptom-diagnosed, our techs test each with a meter rather than chase a code, and carry the common GE lid switches, couplings, and belts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a> guide (all brands), the <a href="/services/washer-repair/ge-error-codes/">GE washer error codes</a> page (for the models that do show them), and <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a>. Sibling: <a href="/services/washer-repair/samsung-not-spinning/">Samsung not spinning</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/washer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/washer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/washer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE washer not spinning: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>GE washer won't spin? We'll pinpoint it by symptom.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

